title: 'State sync: streams chunks directly to database'
doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    State sync writes each verified chunk to the database as it arrives instead of holding the
    whole state in memory (#5053). A restart resumes the download from the recorded cursor.
    `--sync fast-unsafe` is a deprecated alias of `--sync fast`: the state is always downloaded
    with proofs.
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    The sync strategies write each verified chunk through the new
    `sc_client_api::PartialStateImport` trait, implemented by `Client`, which forwards to the
    backend's `PartialStateStore` from the new `Backend::partial_state()` (`None` by default: such
    a backend cannot state-sync). The database records the target and the cursor, so a restart
    resumes the download, and the backend discards an abandoned one. Each node position is written
    exactly once, as parity-db's reference counting needs. The target block imports with the
    `ImportedState` marker, now a unit struct, and `BlockImportOperation::mark_state_synced`.
    `SyncMode::LightState` loses `skip_proofs`, `ProofProvider::verify_range_proof` also returns
    the decoded trie nodes, and the syncing strategies require `PartialStateImport` of the client.
    Supersedes #9247.
